body:
For the purposes of documentation, here are some examples of the behaviors I think the docs should clarify. These all relate to both Find & Rfind interpreting negative values of from in conjunction with what's length, and rfind now being stricter by returning -1 for certain out-of-bounds values of from.

```
“ABCDEF”.find(“DEF”, -1)
// Old Behavior: Don't search, return -1
// New Behavior: Search from D->D, return 3

"ABCDEF".find("BCD", -2)
// Old behavior: Don't search, return -1
// New Behavior: Search C->D, return -1

"ABCDEF".find("BCDE", -2)
// Old behavior: Don't search, return -1
// New Behavior: Search B->C, return 1

"ABCDEF".find("BCD", -3)
// Old behavior: Don't search, return -1
// New Behavior: Search B->D, return 1

"ABCDEF".find("BCD", -5) // A user might think -5 is in bounds as an index, but it is not.
// Old Behavior: Don't search, return -1
// New Behavior: Don't search, return -1

“ABCDEF”.rfind(“DEF”, -2)
// Old behavior: Search D->A, return 3
// New Behavior: Search C->A, return -1

"ABCDEF".rfind("CDEF", -2)
// Old behavior: Search C->A, return 2
// New Behavior: Search B->A, return -1

"ABCDEF".rfind("ABCDEF", -2)
// Old behavior: Search A->A, return 0
// New Behavior: Don't search, return -1

"ABCDEF".rfind("DEF", 4) // A user might think 4 is in bounds as an index, but it is not.
// Old Behavior: Search D->A, return 3
// New Behavior: Don't Search, return -1

"ABCDEF".rfind("DEF", 6)
// Old Behavior: Search D->A, return 3
// New Behavior: Don't search, return -1
```
